==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*teh₂g-|id=touch}}
From {{der|en|xno|ataindre}}, from {{der|en|fro|-}}, from {{der|en|la|attingō}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈteɪn/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-attain.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪn|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[gain]] (an object or desired result).
#: {{syn|en|accomplish|achieve|get}}
#: {{ux|en|To '''attain''' such a high level of proficiency requires hours of practice each day.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2|act=II|scene=iii|text=''Lord Ross.'' Your presence makes us rich, most noble lord.<br>''Lord Willoughby.'' And far surmounts our labour to '''attain''' it.}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Vanity Fair|63|page=572|text={{...}} he will stick at no falsehood, or hesitate at no crime, to '''attain''' his ends.}}
#* {{RQ:Gilbert and Sullivan Mikado|I|page=6|url=https://archive.org/details/entirelyneworigi1885sull/page/6|text={{...}} that’s the highest rank a citizen can '''attain'''!}}
#* '''1937''', {{w|George Orwell}}, ''{{w|The Road to Wigan Pier}},'' New York: Harcourt, Brace, 1958, Part{{nbsp}}1, Chapter{{nbsp}}5, p.{{nbsp}}82,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/roadtowiganpier00geor]</sup>
#*: {{...}} solitude is never easy to '''attain''' in a working-class home
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1964|month=July|author=S. W. Smart|title=The Southern needs no centralised Control|journal=Modern Railways|page=50|text=But an absolutely right time arrival of all trains on the same day is never '''attained'''.}}
#* '''2007''', {{w|Mohsin Hamid}}, ''{{w|The Reluctant Fundamentalist}},'' Orlando: Harcourt, Chapter{{nbsp}}11, p.{{nbsp}}157,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/isbn_9780739488263/page/157]</sup>
#*: Where else could I {{...}} hope to '''attain''' such an impressive income?
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To reach or come to, by progression or motion; to arrive at (a place, time, state, etc.).
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Julius Caesar|V|5|text={{...}} my bones would rest,<br>That have but labour’d to '''attain''' this hour.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|10|line=1026|text=''Canaan'' he now '''attains''',}}
#* {{RQ:Wollstonecraft Vindication|chapter=4|passage=It has also been asserted, by some naturalists, that men do not '''attain''' their full growth and strength till thirty; but that women arrive at maturity by twenty.}}
#* {{RQ:Mary Shelley Frankenstein|1|3|text=the southern gales {{...}} blow us speedily towards those shores which I so ardently desire to '''attain'''}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To come or arrive, by motion, growth, bodily exertion, or efforts toward a place, object, state, etc.
#: {{syn|en|get|reach}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Psalm|139|6|text=Such knowledge is too wonderful for me; it is high, I can not '''attain''' unto it.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Acts|27|12|text=if by any means they might '''attain''' to Phenice}}
#* '''1782''', {{w|William Cowper}}, letter to Joseph Hill dated 11{{nbsp}}November, 1782, in ''Private Correspondence of William Cowper,'' London: Henry Colburn, 1824, Volume{{nbsp}}1, p.{{nbsp}}222,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/privatecorrespon01cowpuoft/page/222]</sup>
#*: You may not, perhaps, live to see your trees '''attain''' to the dignity of timber—I, nevertheless, approve of your planting, and the disinterested spirit that prompts you to it.
#* {{RQ:Scott Lady of the Lake|canto=1|stanza=7|page=10|url=https://archive.org/details/ladylakeapoem07scotgoog/page/n28|text=For, scarce a spear’s length from his haunch,<br>Vindictive toiled the blood-hounds staunch;<br>Nor nearer might the dogs '''attain''',<br>Nor farther might the quarry strain.}}
#* '''1874''', {{w|John Richard Green}}, ''{{w|A Short History of the English People}},'' London: Macmillan, Chapter{{nbsp}}2, Section{{nbsp}}6, p.{{nbsp}}90,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/ashorthistoryen00greegoog/page/n125]</sup>
#*: Few boroughs had as yet '''attained''' to power such as this,
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To get at the knowledge of.
#: {{syn|en|ascertain}}
#* {{RQ:Fuller Church History|section=II|passage={{...}} Master ''{{w|William Camden|Camden}},'' sometimes ''acknowledgeth,'' sometimes ''denieth'' him for an ''English Earle''. Not that I accuse him as inconstant to himself, but suspect my self not well '''attaining''' his meaning therein.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To reach in [[excellence]] or [[degree]].
#: {{syn|en|equal}}
#* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Innovations|page=139|text=Yet notwithstanding as Those that first bring Honour into their Family, are commonly more worthy, then most that succeed: So the first [[precedent|President]] (if it be good) is seldome '''attained''' by Imitation.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[reach]] a person after being [[behind]] them.
#: {{syn|en|catch up with|overtake}}
#* {{RQ:Bacon Henry 7|page-174|text=The ''Earle'' finding {{...}} the enemie retired, pursued with all celeritie into ''Scotland;'' hoping to haue ouer-taken the ''Scottish'' King, and to haue giuen him Battaile; But not '''attaining''' him in time, sate downe before the ''Castle'' of ''Aton'' {{...}} which in a small time hee tooke.}}

